Usage: parmScanner [OPTIONS]

Description:
  Script to generate and run parameter scans based on an OpenFOAM base case.

Options:
  -file <parmFile>      Specify the name of the file containing the list of cases to generate.
  -exec <case_script>   Specify the script to be executed in each case after creation.
  -parallel             Run cases in parallel (background). Default: sequential execution.
  -maxJobs <N>          Maximum number of cases to run simultaneously (only with -parallel).
  -help                 Display this help message and exit.

Example of usage only creating cases (not running them):

  parmScanner -file parmFile.csv

Example of usage creating and running cases in one go:

  parmScanner -file parmFile.csv -exec Allrun -parallel -maxJobs 4

Example of parmFile.csv format:

    scanName                      myParmScan                                      
    baseCase                      myBaseCase                                      
    water.nu                      ddtSchemes.default          nx                  
    constant/transportProperties  system/fvSchemes            system/blockMeshDict
    1e00                          Euler                       20                  
    1e-01                         "CrankNicolson      0.9"    20                 
    1e-02                         "CrankNicolson      0.7"    40                 
    1e-03                         "CrankNicolson      0.6"    40                 
    1e-04                         "CrankNicolson      0.5"    80                 

Notes:
cfdTools must be sourced from bash. It uses bash arrays, which other shells index
differently, so sourcing it from zsh or sh is refused rather than silently misbehaving.
Parameters are set with setDictEntry (documented below), which edits only the line holding
the entry and needs no OpenFOAM installation.
If a scan is named myScan, cases will be placed in myScan/0000 myScan/0001, myScan/0002, ...

A case is run at most once. When -exec is used, parmScanner creates log.<execScript> in the
case as the script is launched, and skips any case in which that file already exists. A scan
can therefore safely be re-run to add new cases: existing cases are left untouched, whether
they finished, are still running or crashed. To re-run a case, delete its log.<execScript>.
The scan can be deleted by executing:

    rm -rf myScan

For numbered list of all cases and their parameter values execute:

    cat myScan/parmScannerParms

If libreoffice is installed, conveniently edit parmFile.csv with the command

    libreoffice --calc parmFile.csv

In the libreoffice import dialog choose "Space" under "Separated by" and choose "Merge delimiters".


Usage: setDictEntry [-syntax <name>] <file> <entry.path> <value>

Description:
  Set a single entry in a dictionary file. Only the line holding the entry is rewritten;
  every other byte of the file is left untouched, so comments, formatting, blank lines and
  full floating point precision are all preserved.

  The entry is addressed by its full path from the top of the file, with sub-dicts separated
  by "." :

    setDictEntry system/blockMeshDict nz 160
    setDictEntry system/fvSchemes ddtSchemes.default "CrankNicolson 0.9"
    setDictEntry system/fvSolution 'solvers."alpha.water.*".nAlphaSubCycles' 4

  The path must match exactly one entry. If it matches none, the paths of any entries with
  the same name are suggested; if it matches several, nothing is written. In both cases the
  file is left unchanged and a non-zero status is returned:

    $ setDictEntry system/fvSchemes default Euler
    setDictEntry: entry "default" not found
                  did you mean "ddtSchemes.default" ?
                  did you mean "gradSchemes.default" ?
                  ...

  Entries appearing inside // or /* */ comments are ignored.

  Only existing entries are modified. Adding and removing entries is deliberately not
  supported, so the base case documents what a scan can vary.

  -syntax <name>  Dictionary dialect. Only "openfoam" (the default) is currently defined:
                  entries "key value;" and sub-dicts "name { ... }". The dialect is
                  described by six tokens (entry terminator, sub-dict open and close, line
                  comment, block comment open and close), so another brace-and-terminator
                  format is a one-line addition. Formats that express nesting through
                  indentation, such as YAML, do not fit this model.

